    category: "ai-drug-discovery",
    summary: "Loop 001 daily digest for 2026-11-09, generated from research-watcher output/latest/hits.jsonl (112 hits across 27 query families, scan regenerated 2026-11-09 06:05 KST via 'bash run.sh scan', hits.jsonl mtime 2026-11-09 06:05, collected_at 2026-11-08T21:03-21:05 UTC). The score-9 band was NOT fully recycled for the first time in fourteen cycles: three brand-new score-9 accessions (GSE350294, GSE350295, GSE350296) topped the scan with pdat 2026/11/07, so today's top pick comes from the score-9 ladder itself rather than from the score-6/4 fallback band. Novelty was verified at two levels: an archive-wide accession check (git log --all -S <GSE> -- src/app/digest/page.tsx returned empty for GSE350294, GSE350295, GSE350296, GSE353716 and GSE325138) and a subject-level keyword cross-check confirming that the biological SUBJECTS of all three signals - a TPD-compatible CRISPR knockout library with a ZMAT2 molecular-glue resistance node, MRTX1133 resistance in pancreatic cancer, and Wnt/beta-catenin-mTOR control of porphyrin clearance in porphyria - have never been featured under any accession in any prior digest commit. All three records were verified against their live NCBI GEO esummary and family-soft records rather than by title matching alone. Signal 1 is the GSE350294/5/6 bundle (Homo sapiens, n=8 Brunello-CSISPR screen, n=18 RNA-seq, n=12 total RNA-seq, pdat 2026/11/07, raw TXT + CSV): targeted protein degradation has become a first-class modality, but genome-wide CRISPR screens run with degraders are frequently dominated by resistance mechanisms that disrupt degrader activity itself - loss of recruited E3 ligase components and broader ubiquitin-proteasome regulators - and the strong selective advantage of those perturbations obscures the less penetrant biological genetic interactions operating downstream of target degradation. The authors solved this by iterative genome-wide screening plus manual curation to build a TPD-compatible knockout library that retains near-genome-scale coverage while excluding a focused set of genes recurrently associated with degrader failure; across multiple degrader screens the library reduced the dominance of UPS-associated resistance mechanisms and improved the detection and prioritization of genetic interactions linked to target biology. Using the RBM39 molecular glue degrader indisulam as the model system, they identified ZMAT2 loss as a resistance mechanism that preserves RBM39 degradation but attenuates the transcriptional and splicing consequences of target depletion - which is the mechanistically interesting result, because it separates 'the drug still works' from 'the downstream biology still happens'. Signal 2 is GSE353716 (Homo sapiens; Mus musculus, n=47, pdat 2026/11/07, raw TAR + CSV + RDS, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Aguirre lab, linked PMID 38975874): a SuperSeries explicitly assembled as 'SuperSeries of: GSE269313' and 'SuperSeries of: GSE269985' (both of which are themselves NOVEL against the archive), titled 'Mechanisms of resistance to oncogenic KRAS inhibition in pancreatic cancer'. The visible sample manifest is a matched parental-versus-MRTX1133-resistant design replicated across three human pancreatic lines (PANC-1, Panc 02.03, 6499C4 / 6419C5) with three replicates per condition, plus KPC mouse models with snRNA-seq on resistant and vehicle arms - so the design isolates drug resistance rather than cell-line heterogeneity, and spans human and murine. Signal 3 is GSE325138 (Mus musculus, n=20, pdat 2026/09/11, raw CSV + TIFF + TXT + XLSX): porphyrias are rare metabolic disorders of heme biosynthesis causing toxic porphyrin accumulation, oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction and liver injury; prior work showed hepatocyte-specific beta-catenin deletion attenuates porphyrin accumulation and liver injury in DDC-induced porphyria, and this study tests whether inhibiting Wnt/beta-catenin-glutamine-synthesis signalling reduces heme synthesis and simultaneously enhances porphyrin clearance via autophagy and improved mitochondrial quality control. Readouts are unusually broad for a GEO deposit - spatial transcriptomics, targeted metabolomics, immunohistochemistry, confocal mt-Keima mitophagy imaging, high-resolution respirometry and transmission electron microscopy - and the study includes a human arm, examining human liver biopsies and explants from porphyria patients by dual-label immunohistochemistry, with patient samples mirroring the murine findings (heme enzymes and beta-catenin increased in porphyria cutanea tarda, autophagy inversely correlated with beta-catenin expression). The unifying thesis of the day is that the highest-value public assets are not discovery atlases but perturbation-designed experiments whose controls are built in, and whose output is a short list of named, druggable nodes: a knockout library that makes a resistance screen legible (ZMAT2), a parental-versus-resistant pairing that makes a resistance programme legible (MRTX1133), and a deletion-plus-inhibition design that makes a clearance mechanism legible (Wnt/GS-mTOR, autophagy, mitophagy). A second thesis worth carrying is that two of today's three signals are GEO-depositor-abstract stage only, so the mechanistic claims are not yet peer reviewed, and that the correct brief posture is a decision-ready hypothesis with a stated validation ladder rather than a citable conclusion. A process note carried forward rather than patched silently: the watcher's modality labels are still visibly noisy on fresh records - the three TPD records are tagged 'protein-design' although GSE350295 and GSE350296 are RNA-seq arms of a CRISPR screen, GSE353716 is tagged 'mixed' although it is a clean snRNA-seq SuperSeries, and GSE325138 is tagged 'protein-design' although it is a spatial-transcriptomics plus metabolomics study - and the per-record scorer still rewards raw-file availability and licence cleanliness over mechanistic specificity, so a mechanism-specificity component in the rubric plus a freshness-keyed query rotation remain the two concrete watcher improvements worth making.",
