    category: "bioinformatics",
    summary: "Loop 001 daily digest for 2026-11-08, generated from research-watcher output/latest/hits.jsonl (112 hits across 27 query families, scan regenerated 2026-11-08 06:02 KST via bash run.sh scan, collected_at 2026-11-07T21:01-21:03 UTC). The score-9 band was once again fully recycled - the same long-standing top ladder (TNBC CD45+ immune atlas, sequential lymph-node-metastasis spatial record, Oprm1-A118G opioid-dependence study, the lung and colorectal CosMx resources, the post-CAR-T marrow spatial atlas, the HNSCC dependency map and the 3D-chromatin-GWAS bundle) has topped every scan since early October - so the actionable harvest came again from the score-6 and score-4 bands. Novelty was verified at two levels: an archive-wide accession check (git log --all -S <GSE> -- src/app/digest/page.tsx returned empty for GSE353325, GSE301733 and GSE328462) and a subject-level check confirming that the biological SUBJECTS of all three signals - CLIC1-mediated high-ROS/high-GSH redox remodeling in metastatic colorectal cancer, CD4+ T cell proteogenomic remodelling in MASH liver, and shared-versus-distinct ALS-versus-ageing muscle degeneration programs - have never been featured under any accession in any prior digest commit. All three records were verified against their live NCBI GEO full-text records rather than by title matching alone. Signal 1 is GSE353325 (Homo sapiens, n=9, pdat 2026/11/06, raw TAR + expression CSV + three gseapy prerank reports, score 6): the depositor summary describes a mechanistically complete, previously unexplained state - metastatic cancer cells persist under chronic oxidative stress while maintaining elevated antioxidant defences, and the stress-responsive chloride channel transmembrane CLIC1 is the mediator; chronic oxidative stress induces CLIC1 translocation to the plasma membrane, releasing glutathione into the cytoplasm and sustaining ROS production by increasing chloride conductance, which permits the stable coexistence of high ROS and high GSH; inducing this redox state in non-metastatic CLIC1-expressing cells is sufficient to confer aggressive behaviour in vitro and in vivo, while genetic silencing or antibody-mediated inhibition of tmCLIC1 markedly suppresses tumour growth and metastatic spread across zebrafish and murine models including immunocompetent orthotopic settings; the design contrasts SW480 primary colorectal cells treated with N-acetylcysteine against SW620 metastatic cells from the same patient, so the redox axis is isolated by construction rather than by correlation. Signal 2 is GSE301733 (Mus musculus, n=4, pdat 2026/05/18, CITE-seq + scRNA-seq, Hepatology 2026): hepatic CD4+ T cells were FACS-purified from PBS-flushed mouse livers to remove circulating lymphocytes, with five animals pooled per condition, giving a proteogenomic single-cell atlas of intrahepatic CD4+ T cells in healthy versus steatohepatitis; the atlas shows a marked shift in CD4+ T cell composition in MASH with enrichment of Th1, regulatory and cytotoxic subsets, identifies Tnfrsf4 (OX40) upregulation in hepatic CD4+ T cells during MASH, and the peer-reviewed paper frames this as a CD4+ T cell-dependent immunopathogenic circuit promising as an immunotherapeutic target to alleviate MASH and liver fibrosis. Signal 3 is GSE328462 (Mus musculus, n=10, pdat 2026/10/15, snRNA-seq, PNAS 2026): an integrated single-nucleus transcriptomic atlas of tibialis anterior muscle built from WT mice at 6, 16 and 120 weeks against SOD1-G93A ALS mice at 6 and 16 weeks, with two independent biological replicates per condition, which delineates the shared and the divergent molecular programs of ALS-driven and age-related muscle wasting and is positioned by the authors as a resource for identifying targets tailored to each condition. The unifying thesis of the day is that a commercially useful asset is a ranked hub list extracted from a deliberately constrained transcriptome - a single ion channel in metastatic colorectal cancer, a single receptor in fibrotic liver, a shared-versus-divergent program split in ageing muscle - and that all three are already public with raw files. A second thesis worth carrying is that two of the three signals have crossed the preprint-to-journal boundary (Hepatology and PNAS), so the mechanistic claims are externally peer reviewed and can be cited in a client brief without hedging, whereas only the CLIC1 record is still at the GEO-depositor-abstract stage. A standing process note carried forward rather than patched silently: the score-9 band has now been saturated for thirteen consecutive cycles, the watcher's modality labels are visibly unreliable on fresh records - GSE353325 is tagged metabolism but was surfaced by the OpenFold weights query, and GSE301733 is tagged protein-design although it is a CITE-seq immune atlas surfaced by the MASH liver atlas query - and the per-record scorer still rewards raw-file availability and licence cleanliness over mechanistic specificity, so a freshness-keyed query rotation and a mechanism-specificity component in the rubric remain the two concrete improvements worth making to the watcher",
